<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<definitions xmlns="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL"
             xmlns:flowable="http://flowable.org/bpmn"
             targetNamespace="http://yesmadam.in/botin">

  <!--
    FORGET_MPIN — the first real concern, and the simplest possible shape a
    concern can have: start, one Service Task, end.

    It is a T0. The partner is shown where to reset their own MPIN and the
    session ends. NOTHING ELSE HAPPENS. No ticket row, no action row, no agent.
    That is not a simplification for the POC — 100% of this concern's volume is
    deflectable, and the old SOP had an agent clicking Reset MPIN on the
    partner's behalf, which is the work this removes.

    Why a process engine at all for three elements: because the concern's shape
    is declared here rather than in Java, every concern is read the same way, and
    the ones that follow — gateways on tier, gateways on a kill switch, a User
    Task for agent connect — are additions to this file rather than rewrites of a
    service class.

    finaliseStep is the LAST thing to run. It writes nextStep onto the
    help_session row. It must not leave anything the client needs in process
    variables: the instant this instance completes, its ACT_RU_* rows are
    deleted and those variables are gone.
  -->

  <process id="forget-mpin" name="Forget MPIN — T0 deflection" isExecutable="true">

    <startEvent id="start"/>
    <sequenceFlow id="f1" sourceRef="start" targetRef="finaliseStep"/>

    <serviceTask id="finaliseStep" name="Write the deflection step"
                 flowable:delegateExpression="${forgetMpinDelegate}"/>
    <sequenceFlow id="f2" sourceRef="finaliseStep" targetRef="end"/>

    <endEvent id="end"/>

  </process>
</definitions>
